    <body>
      <sec id="article-28433.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pediatric postcardiac arrest syndrome remains a major contributor to morbidity and mortality despite return of spontaneous circulation. Mortality remains high due to a combination of recurrent cardiac arrest, hemodynamic instability, myocardial dysfunction, systemic ischemia&#x02013;reperfusion injury, and secondary hypoxic-ischemic brain injury. Cardiac arrest in children most commonly arises from noncardiac etiologies, including respiratory failure, shock, sepsis, arrhythmias, electrolyte abnormalities, and severe acidosis. Following resuscitation, affected children frequently demonstrate hypotension, impaired tissue perfusion, altered mental status, and evolving multiorgan dysfunction. Effective postresuscitation care requires rapid identification of ongoing physiologic derangements and prompt initiation of targeted supportive therapies to stabilize cardiovascular, respiratory, and neurologic function.</p>
        <p>This activity equips clinicians with advanced knowledge and practical skills for the systematic evaluation and management of pediatric patients after return of spontaneous circulation. Participants learn to perform structured postresuscitation assessments, optimize oxygenation and ventilation, manage hypotension and myocardial dysfunction with vasoactive support, and implement neuroprotective strategies, including targeted temperature management. Emphasis is placed on continuous monitoring, early recognition of complications, and identification of the underlying cause of arrest to prevent recurrence. The activity highlights the importance of coordinated interprofessional collaboration among critical care clinicians, nurses, respiratory therapists, pharmacists, and neurologic specialists. Effective communication within this team-based framework supports timely intervention, reduces secondary injury, and improves survival and neurologic outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assess pediatric patients for clinical and diagnostic features of postcardiac arrest syndrome, including hemodynamic instability, neurologic impairment, and laboratory derangements.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Develop protocols for continuous, multiparameter monitoring in pediatric patients with postcardiac arrest&#x000a0;syndrome to enable early detection of physiologic disturbances and guide timely interventions.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based approaches to deliver personalized postresuscitation care, thereby minimizing complications and improving survival.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with all members of the interprofessional team, including specialists such as pediatric intensivists, neurologists, and rehabilitation physicians, to provide efficient, comprehensive, and coordinated care.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28433&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28433">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28433.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>More than 20,000 pediatric cardiac arrests occur annually in the United States (US).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r3">[3]</xref> In the pediatric population, cardiac arrest typically results from progressive respiratory compromise or shock, with nonshockable rhythms such as asystole or pulseless electrical activity occurring more frequently than primary arrhythmias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Mortality following pediatric cardiac arrest and return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC) remains greater than 90%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r5">[5]</xref> ROSC is defined as restoration of sustained perfusion, achieved either through an effective native cardiac rhythm or extracorporeal support using extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO). Initiation of ECMO during ongoing cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) is termed "extracorporeal CPR" (ECPR). Improving outcomes after pediatric resuscitation requires addressing key elements of postarrest management to limit secondary organ injury.</p>
        <p>Resuscitation from cardiac arrest is commonly followed by the development of postcardiac arrest syndrome (PCAS), which may progress over days to weeks after sustained circulation is achieved. This syndrome comprises hypoxic&#x02013;ischemic brain injury, myocardial dysfunction, a systemic ischemia&#x02013;reperfusion response, and ongoing effects of the inciting pathology. Pediatric postcardiac arrest management emphasizes early anticipation, recognition, and treatment of this complex physiologic state to improve survival and neurologic outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Epidemiology</bold>
</p>
        <p>The timing and severity of PCAS phases may differ between out-of-hospital (OHCA) and in-hospital (IHCA) cardiac arrest.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;An estimated&#x000a0;5000 children experience OHCA annually in the US, whereas approximately 6000 infants and children develop IHCA each year.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;After initial resuscitation from OHCA and admission to a pediatric intensive care unit (ICU), many children die during the postcardiac arrest period prior to discharge.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Despite improvements in rates of ROSC and overall survival following pediatric cardiac arrest, PCAS affects more than 6000 children annually in the US. The primary objective of pediatric postcardiac arrest care (PCAC) is to improve survival to hospital discharge and optimize survival with favorable neurological outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Data collected from 2005 to 2013 indicate overall survival rates ranging from 6.4% to 10.2%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r16">[16]</xref> Despite updates to postresuscitation management recommendations, survival to hospital discharge after OHCA has not changed significantly over the past decade. Most children who survive cardiac arrest sustain short- and long-term neurologic morbidities.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28433.s3" sec-type="Function">
        <title>Function</title>
        <p>
<bold>Phases of Postcardiac Arrest Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>PCAS management requires multisystem support throughout each phase (see <bold>Image</bold>. Postcardiac Arrest Care Phases). The goals of care during all phases focus on limiting ongoing injury.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Overview of&#x000a0;<bold>Postcardiac Arrest&#x000a0;</bold>Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>During the post-cardiac arrest period, the healthcare team must anticipate and assess evolving systemic and organ dysfunction and proactively support organ function. Aggressive supportive care should be applied during this phase to maintain systemic perfusion and organ function.&#x000a0;Goal-directed therapy represents a central component of pediatric PCAC, with multimodal targeting of key physiologic domains, particularly hemodynamic stability and adequate oxygenation and ventilation, likely providing greater benefit than isolated interventions. Pediatric cardiac arrest is associated with substantial morbidity and mortality.&#x000a0;Factors linked to more favorable outcomes include in-hospital arrest, shorter arrest duration, prompt initiation of CPR, and hypothermia as the precipitating cause.</p>
        <p>In contrast, postresuscitation hypotension and prolonged resuscitative efforts correlate with a worse prognosis. Current management strategies prioritize avoiding hypotension and individualized consideration of targeted temperature management (TTM) and other PCAS-directed therapies (see <bold>Table.</bold> Post-Cardiac Arrest Syndrome Monitoring Protocols).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;table below lists the essential monitoring components for postcardiac arrest syndrome (PCAS), categorized into general, additional hemodynamic, and neurological monitoring. The parameters include continuous physiological monitoring, such as pulse oximetry and core temperature, as well as serial clinical examinations and advanced imaging, such as brain computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-28433.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
