        <p>
<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>PaCO2,&#x000a0;arterial partial pressure of carbon dioxide;<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>PaO2, arterial partial pressure of oxygen</p>
        <p>
<bold>Therapeutic Hypothermia and Targeted Temperature Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>The 2019&#x000a0;International Liaison Committee on Resuscitation Pediatric&#x000a0;Consensus on Science and Treatment Recommendations summarized evidence supporting the use of TTM, maintaining body temperature between 32 &#x000b0;C and 34 &#x000b0;C, after IHCA or OHCA in infants, children, and adolescents&#x000a0;younger than 18.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r19">[19]</xref> Continuous monitoring of core body temperature is required throughout TTM. In comatose infants and children aged 24 hours to 18 years following OHCA or IHCA, temperature may be managed with an initial hypothermic TTM phase at 32 &#x000b0;C to 34 &#x000b0;C, followed by controlled normothermia at 36 &#x000b0;C to 37.5 &#x000b0;C, or with normothermic TTM alone at 36 &#x000b0;C to 37.5 &#x000b0;C. Optimal TTM duration remains undefined. In the&#x000a0;Therapeutic Hypothermia After Pediatric Cardiac Arrest trials, strategies included 48 hours at 32 &#x000b0;C to 34 &#x000b0;C followed by 72 hours at 36 &#x000b0;C to 37.5 &#x000b0;C, or a continuous 5-day period of TTM maintained at 36&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C to 37.5 &#x000b0;C.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>Adequate analgesia and sedation are essential to maintain comfort and suppress shivering and may be supported with neuromuscular blockade when necessary. Opioids and benzodiazepines are commonly employed for effective pain control and sedation. Rewarming should be performed incrementally at approximately 0.25&#x000a0;&#x000b0;C to 0.5 &#x000b0;C/h to reduce the risk of hyperthermia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Elevated body temperature during PCAC requires active management, as temperatures of 38 &#x000b0;C or greater are associated with higher mortality. Excessive cooling below 32 &#x000b0;C should be avoided to minimize the risk of adverse neurologic outcomes.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hemodynamic Optimization</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hemodynamic support represents a cornerstone of PCAC, as inadequate perfusion strongly correlates with poor outcomes. Recommended approaches to vasoactive therapy and circulatory stabilization are described below.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Blood pressure</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hemodynamic instability after ROSC is common in children. Hypotension, defined as systolic&#x000a0;blood pressure below the fifth percentile, after pediatric cardiac arrest, is associated with lower survival to hospital discharge.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r23">[23]</xref> Fluid boluses and vasoactive agents should be administered according to guidelines.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>No pediatric interventional studies have evaluated the effect of manipulating blood pressure on survival after ROSC.&#x000a0;However, poor perfusion is consistently associated with increased morbidity and mortality, as evidenced by both anecdotal observations and data analyses. Therefore, vasoactive therapy is recommended and should be tailored to each patient. No specific blood pressure target has been established, and vasopressors may need to be adjusted to optimize perfusion while avoiding excessive myocardial workload.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Circulatory support</bold>
</p>
        <p>Epinephrine infusion is the preferred agent for postarrest hypotension in pediatric patients. Early administration, within 15 minutes of OHCA, correlates with higher survival and improved neurological outcomes in children with nontraumatic, nonshockable cardiac arrest.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Epinephrine increases blood pressure through peripheral vasoconstriction and exerts inotropic and chronotropic effects. Other vasoactive agents, including dopamine, norepinephrine, and dobutamine, may support blood pressure but are generally reserved as second-line therapies or used selectively in specific conditions, such as renal failure or cardiogenic shock.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Oxygenation and Ventilation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Oxygenation and ventilation status represent essential components of postarrest assessment. Following ROSC, targeting a normal partial pressure of&#x000a0;arterial oxygen is reasonable, with oxygen goals individualized for underlying conditions such as cyanotic congenital heart disease. The fraction of inspired oxygen should be maintained at the lowest level necessary to achieve an oxygen saturation between 94% and 99%. Hypoxemia should be avoided&#x000a0;in PCAC, particularly&#x000a0;during periods of oxygen adjustment. Oxygenation status should be monitored using continuous cardiac monitoring, continuous pulse oximetry, end-tidal carbon dioxide measurements, and intermittent arterial blood gases every 10&#x000a0;to 15 minutes to guide oxygen titration and support mechanical ventilation.</p>
        <p>Post-ROSC ventilation strategies should aim for normocapnia, defined as a&#x000a0;partial pressure of arterial carbon dioxide (PaCO2) appropriate for the child&#x02019;s physiology, typically 35&#x000a0;to 45 mm Hg, or a target tailored to underlying conditions. Prolonged periods of significant hypercapnia or hypocapnia should be avoided.&#x000a0;Although the relationship between PaCO2 and partial pressure of end-tidal carbon dioxide (PetCO2) can support noninvasive monitoring, PetCO2 should be interpreted with caution, as variations in cardiac output and increased alveolar dead space may&#x000a0;weaken&#x000a0;the correlation with arterial values.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Arrhythmia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Rhythm disturbances observed during the postcardiac arrest period include premature atrial and ventricular contractions, supraventricular tachycardias, and ventricular tachycardias. Evidence is insufficient to support routine prophylactic administration of antiarrhythmics after ROSC.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Persistent arrhythmias following ROSC should be managed pharmacologically according to the underlying cardiac anatomy and myocardial function. Consultation with pediatric cardiac electrophysiology specialists is often required. Many vasoactive agents used to support myocardial function can increase myocardial irritability and the risk of arrhythmias.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Mechanical circulatory support with ECMO may be considered during PCAC when significant cardiorespiratory instability persists despite appropriate medical management. If ECMO is initiated during CPR (ECPR), support is typically continued into the postcardiac arrest phase until safe and reliable weaning from mechanical support is possible. The 2015 American Heart Association guidelines recommend consideration of ECPR for pediatric patients with IHCA and an underlying cardiac diagnosis. Overall, the clinical effectiveness of ECMO after ROSC remains inconclusive. Studies indicate ECMO may improve survival for rewarming and circulatory support in cold-water drowning victims.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Neurological Monitoring and Seizure Management After Return of Spontaneous Circulation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Serial neurological examinations can identify evolving hypoxic-ischemic brain injury. Due to the high incidence of subclinical seizures post-ROSC, monitoring with an electroencephalogram (EEG) supports therapy guidance and detection of evolving injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r27">[27]</xref> Neuroimaging can assist in identifying primary cerebral etiologies of cardiac arrest and in assessing the extent of severe brain injury.</p>
        <p>Brain computed tomography can identify treatable intracranial causes of cardiac arrest but has limited utility for predicting neurological prognosis. Brain magnetic resonance imaging performed after ROSC, together with serial neurological examinations, EEG, and adjunctive assessments, may help characterize neurological injury and potential recovery. Acute&#x000a0;seizures detected on EEG are treated with benzodiazepines, levetiracetam, or phenytoin. Some seizures may be refractory to treatment.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Glucose Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>No interventional clinical trials of glucose control have focused exclusively on children during PCAC. Both hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia are associated with unfavorable outcomes in children and adults with critical illness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r30">[30]</xref> During the postresuscitation period, clinicians should promptly identify, prevent, and treat hypoglycemia (&#x02264;45 mg/dL in the newborn and &#x02264;60 mg/dL in the child) to avoid further neurologic insult. Severe hyperglycemia can exacerbate postcardiac arrest volume depletion and hemodynamic instability.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28433.s4" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>The Pediatric Advanced Life Support (PALS) guidelines have been revised repeatedly to improve survival after cardiac arrest. The most recent update was published in 2018.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28433.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Prognosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Favorable outcomes after cardiac arrest are more commonly associated with shorter arrest duration, prompt initiation of CPR, hypothermia as the precipitating cause, and arrests occurring in-hospital. Factors associated with unfavorable neurologic outcomes following OHCA include&#x000a0;younger age, sudden infant death syndrome, and blunt trauma. Factors linked to decreased survival after IHCA include older age, preexisting conditions, interventions such as tracheal intubation, mechanical ventilation, and vasopressor use at the time of arrest, as well as arrests occurring during night or weekend shifts. In both OHCA and IHCA, prearrest rhythms of bradycardia, ventricular fibrillation, or tachycardia are associated with the highest survival, while&#x000a0;pulseless electrical activity shows higher survival than asystole.</p>
        <p>Beyond the initial reperfusion phase, several factors may compromise cerebral oxygen delivery and contribute to secondary brain injury in the hours to days following cardiac arrest. These factors include hypotension, hypoxemia, impaired cerebrovascular autoregulation, and brain edema. Hyperglycemia is common after cardiac arrest and correlates with poor neurological outcomes following OHCA.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28433.s5" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>PCAC is typically initiated in the emergency department and continues in the ICU for children who achieve survival. This phase of care is resource-intensive and requires coordination among a broad interprofessional team, including intensivists, neurologists, primary care clinicians, ICU nursing staff, social workers, ethics consultants, organ donation services, and spiritual care providers.&#x000a0;Child protective services may also be involved in selected cases.</p>
        <p>Given the high mortality associated with cardiac arrest and the substantial risk of poor neurological outcomes among survivors, early and transparent communication with families is essential. Early involvement of ethics teams and bereavement support services is recommended. Survivors of pediatric cardiac arrest frequently sustain hypoxic-ischemic brain injury with long-term impairments in daily functioning, highlighting the importance of comprehensive discussions regarding potential outcomes. Regular updates and continuous communication from the clinical team support family understanding of the clinical trajectory and preparation for possible outcomes. An interprofessional care model is critical, as postcardiac arrest management places substantial demands on healthcare resources and contributes to provider stress while presenting emotional challenges for families and care teams. Coordinated and integrated responses from care providers across prehospital, emergency department, and ICU settings may improve outcomes for initial survivors.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28433.s6">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28433&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28433">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
